Arca
====

Arca lists, extracts and makes archives on AROS. It comes as two programs
over one core: Arca, a Zune program, and arca-cli, a Shell command. Two
helper programs come with them and are started when needed: unarc-7z reads
7z, RAR and ZIP with 7-Zip's own code, and unarc-lha makes LhA archives.

@TARGET@

Mainline ABIv1 packages (PC and Raspberry Pi) require nightly 2026-11-06
or later with stdc.library 0.53.


Installing
----------

Copy the Arca drawer anywhere, for example to SYS:Utilities or to a drawer
whose name has spaces. Nothing else is installed and nothing outside the
drawer is changed, apart from ENVARC:Arca.prefs when you change a setting.
Keep unarc-7z and unarc-lha in the same drawer as Arca and arca-cli. To
remove Arca, delete the drawer (and ENVARC:Arca.prefs).

Arca was previously tested as Unarc.
It reads Unarc.prefs if Arca.prefs is absent and saves changes as Arca.prefs.
The old preferences are left intact. Existing archive icons that name Unarc
must be changed to Arca. The helper filenames remain unarc-7z and unarc-lha.

The GUI uses Zune and the NList, NListview and NFloattext classes that come
with AROS. If one of them is missing, Arca names it when it starts.

No file type is associated with Arca. To open archives with it from
Wanderer, give an archive a project icon whose default tool is Arca, or set
Arca as the default tool of the archive types in Wanderer's preferences.


The Zune program
----------------

Started on its own, Arca opens its start window. Choose the format with
the button at the top (LhA, ZIP, 7z or tar.gz) and the compression with the
slider, then drop files or drawers on the marked area: they are packed at
once, next to where they are. "Each item into its own archive" makes one
archive per dropped item. An archive dropped on the window is extracted
instead. Packing first counts what was dropped ("Preparing..."), then shows
how much of it is packed; Cancel works in both steps and leaves no archive.

When a job ends, the window shows a short summary: what was done, the
drawer and any notes. "Details..." shows the whole text, with full paths and
every message.

"Pack..." does the same through a file requester: choose files, or press OK
in a drawer with no file chosen to pack the whole drawer. "Open archive..."
shows an archive's contents first.

When an archive of the same name already exists, Arca offers a new name
("Website 2.zip"), which Return chooses; replacing the old one needs its own
button. The old archive is removed only after the new one is complete. If it
cannot be removed (something has it open), it stays as "<name>.old" and the
result says so.

Started with an archive (a double-click on an archive whose default tool is
Arca), it extracts the archive next to itself into a new drawer and shows
what happened. Settings, or the tool type MODE=BROWSE, can show the contents
first instead. When a file to extract is already there, Arca asks: it
shows the file's name, its drawer and both sizes and dates, and Return
skips the file; replacing it needs its own button.

Settings... has three pages. General: what to show at start, what a
double-click on an archive does, whether to open the drawer with the result
and whether to close the result window by itself (only when there is nothing
to report; warnings and errors always stay open). Packing: where archives
are saved (next to the items, ask each time, or one drawer) and how several
items are named. Extracting: where to extract, when to make a new drawer,
and what to do when a file exists (ask, skip it, or keep both; files on disk
are never replaced without asking).

In the browser you can select entries and extract only those, extract everything,
or drag the selected entries onto an open Wanderer drawer window. Amiga+A
selects every entry. Several archives dropped at once are extracted one
after another; the progress window lists them and ends with one summary.

When a file already exists, Arca asks; Return skips the file, and replacing
it needs its own button. Cancel keeps the files already finished and removes
the one in progress. Nothing that was there before is overwritten unless you
say so.


The Shell command
-----------------

    arca-cli <archive>                 extract next to the archive
    arca-cli <archive> TO Work:Out     extract into Work:Out
    arca-cli <archive> LIST            show the contents, write nothing
    arca-cli new.lha PACK <files>      make an archive (.lha, .zip, .tar.gz, .7z)
    arca-cli HELP                      all options, with examples
    arca-cli FORMATS                   what each format supports

Return codes: 0 done, 5 done with notes (something skipped or renamed),
10 partly done, cancelled or stopped by an error, 20 nothing done. Ctrl-C
stops the command; finished files are kept.


Formats
-------

Extracting: ZIP (also ZIP64), 7z, RAR 1.5 to 5, LhA,
LZX, DMS, tar, tar.gz, tar.bz2, tar.xz, gzip, bzip2, xz, ISO 9660 and more.
Making: LhA, ZIP, tar.gz and 7z.

Multi-volume sets (name.part1.rar, name.rar + name.r00, name.7z.001) can be
opened through any of their volumes. A set with a volume missing is refused
before anything is written, and the message names the missing volume.

Archives with Amiga protection bits and comments (LhA, LZX) keep them, and
LhA archives made by Arca store them too.


Passwords
---------

Encrypted 7z, RAR and ZIP archives (ZipCrypto and AES) are read through
unarc-7z. When the list of entries itself is encrypted, Arca asks for the
password at once; when only the data is, before anything is written. The
Shell command asks without showing what you type; in scripts, give it with
PASSWORD. Without a password, encrypted entries are left out and reported.
The password is not saved or logged anywhere.


unarc-7z
--------

unarc-7z is 7-Zip 26.04 (7z, RAR and ZIP handlers only), built for AROS as a
program of its own. Arca uses it for:

- every RAR archive;
- encrypted 7z and ZIP archives;
- 7z and ZIP archives in a method the built-in reader lacks (for example
  Deflate64);
- 7z archives split into several files.

Plain 7z and ZIP archives are read by the built-in reader, which is faster.
Without unarc-7z, Arca still reads 7z, RAR and ZIP as far as the built-in
reader can, without passwords, and says so when an archive needs unarc-7z.
An unarc-7z from another Arca release is not used; install both from one
package. Without it, some file times from RAR 4 archives can be off by your
time zone.


unarc-lha
---------

unarc-lha is LHa for UNIX 1.14i in its Amiga version (by Ola Söder), with a
few changes of ours, built as a program of its own. Arca and arca-cli use
it to make LhA archives: header level 1 as Amiga LhA writes it, with file
and drawer comments, all protection bits (h, s, p, a and rwed), dates to the
second, and empty drawers. Its archives open with LhA on any Amiga, with XAD
and with LHa for UNIX. "Store only" stores (lh0), "Normal" packs (lh5).

LhA packing refuses a volume as a whole (choose the drawers and files on it),
a drawer that would contain the archive itself, and a tree with links in it
(LhA would follow them); use ZIP or 7z for those. Without unarc-lha, Arca
makes ZIP, 7z and tar.gz archives as before.


Known limitations
-----------------

- Archive files over 2 GB: files over 2 GB inside an archive extract
  correctly, and the volumes of a RAR set are found whatever their size.
  But on SFS volumes (the usual AROS system disk) a file over 2 GB cannot be
  read from anywhere but its start: SFS refuses every seek in such a file.
  So a 7z or ZIP archive that is itself over 2 GB cannot be opened there.
  Keep such archives under 2 GB, or split them into volumes.
- Names are shown and written in ISO-8859-1, AROS's character set. A name
  with characters outside it is changed, and Arca lists such changes before
  extracting.
- Links stored in archives are not created; they are listed as not
  extracted.
- Disk images in archives (DMS) are listed but not written to a disk.


Licences
--------

Arca's own code is under the MIT licence. It contains libarchive, XAD,
liblzma, zlib, bzip2 and GNU libiconv, each under its own licence; XAD and
GNU libiconv are under the GNU LGPL 2.1, which lets you rebuild Arca with a
changed version of them. unarc-7z contains 7-Zip under the GNU LGPL 2.1, and
its RAR code comes with the unRAR licence restriction: it may not be used to
re-create the RAR compression algorithm.

unarc-lha is under the terms of LHa for UNIX (Licences/LHa-for-UNIX-terms.txt).
They allow it to be passed on only with its source, which is in the Source
drawer together with our changes; a distribution on a disc or other medium
that is not the Internet should tell LHa's authors; and there are conditions
for commercial use. Read the terms before you put unarc-lha on a medium or
into something you sell.

The full texts are in the Licences drawer, and THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES there
lists what is used where.

This release comes from the Arca source tree at the revision named
in the file Revision; the sources of the libraries are the upstream releases
named in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.
